  "details": "Currently, some translation strings are shared across multiple places in the UI. This includes a few cases, where the following lines are used both as a title for a **setting toggle** and as a title for a collapsible **settings sub-category**:\n\n- 23119 “Graphics Quality”\n\n- 23219 “Face Mirror” and 23217 “Face Mirror{ln}” (the difference and usage of these two are not clear, and why {ln} there?)\n\n- 23193 “Personal Mirror”\n\n- 23077 “Earmuff Mode”\n\nThe problem is that the guidelines and rules for titles and regular lines may differ in some languages, so the translated strings might differ, either in style or in preferred words.\n\nFor example, in Russian, **Camel Case** (which is widely used in English) is **exotic** and rarely used. For highlighting titles, FULLY CAPITALIZED LETTERS are usually used, but this looks **ugly** on most UIs. We accepted the guideline to use regular capitalization (with only the first capitalized letter in the sentence) in all cases (including lines for settings), except for Action Buttons and Titles, where we use Camel Case matching the original English style.\n\nSo, we need to have “Качество Графики” in one place and “Качество графики” in another to keep **consistent style** across entire UI.\n\nWe ask you to **separate those strings** and all other similar cases in the future to achieve more freedom for translators.",
